The final match of the [[2002 FIFA World Cup|2002 World Cup]], the 17th edition of [[FIFA]]'s competition for national [[association football|football]] teams, was played at the [[International Stadium Yokohama|International Stadium]] in [[Yokohama]], Japan, on 30 June 2002, and was contested by [[Germany national football team|Germany]] and [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]]. 

The tournament comprised hosts [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]] and [[Japan national football team|Japan]], holders [[France national football team|France]], and 29 other teams who emerged from [[2002 FIFA World Cup qualification|the qualification phase]], organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]].  The 32 teams competed in a group stage, from which 16 teams qualified for [[2002 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the knockout stage]]. En route to the final, Germany finished first in [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group E|Group E]], with two wins and a draw, after which they defeated [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] in the round of 16, the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] in the quarter-finals and South Korea in the semi-finals. Brazil finished top of [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group C|Group C]] with three wins, before defeating [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] in the round of 16, [[England national football team|England]] in the quarter-final, and [[Turkey national football team|Turkey]] in the semi-final. The final took place in front of 69,029 supporters, with an estimated 1.1&nbsp;billion watching on television, and was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Pierluigi Collina]] from Italy.

After a goalless first half, Brazil took the lead through [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]] on 67 minutes, scoring after what German goalkeeper [[Oliver Kahn]] called his "only mistake in the finals". They extended their lead twelve minutes later when [[José Kléberson|Kléberson]] ran towards the German [[penalty area]] before passing towards [[Rivaldo]]. He let the ball pass through his legs and it reached Ronaldo, who used his first touch to take the ball away from German player [[Gerald Asamoah]], and then shot the ball into the bottom corner of Kahn's net. The final score was 2–0 to Brazil.

Brazil's win was their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|fifth World Cup title]], which remains a record {{as of|2026|lc=yes}}. Ronaldo was named the [[Player of the match|man of the match]], while Kahn was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Golden Ball]] as FIFA's outstanding player of the tournament. Brazil's [[Manager (association football)|manager]], [[Luiz Felipe Scolari]], expressed "the joy of knowing we did our job", while noting that it was "full of very hard work". His German counterpart [[Rudi Völler]] said, "When you lose a game, the disappointment is great, of course. But it is no shame to lose against a team like Brazil." At the next World Cup in Germany in [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006]], Brazil were eliminated in the quarter-final by [[France national football team|France]] while Germany reached the semi-final stage on home soil and eventually finished third.

As of 2026, 2002's Brazil is the last team to win every World Cup game on the way to the title.

==Background==
[[File:Deutsches Fußballmuseum 2015 2-Fevernova.jpg|thumb|right|An example of the Adidas Fevernova ball used in the match|alt=View of the match ball]]
The [[2002 FIFA World Cup]] was the 17th edition of the [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s football competition for national teams, held in Japan and South Korea between 31 May and 30 June 2002.<ref name="Britanica">{{Cite encyclopedia |url-status=live |title=World Cup: History & Winners |last1=Tikkanen |first1=Amy |last2=Augustyn |first2=Adam |last3=Levy |first3=Michael |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230905070057/https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football |archive-date=5 September 2023 |last4=Ray |first4=Michael |last5=Luebering |first5=J. E. |last6=Lotha |first6=Gloria |last7=Young |first7=Grace |last8=Shepherd |first8=Melinda C. |last9=Sinha |first9=Surabhi |last10=Rodriguez |first10=Emily |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=4 September 2023 |access-date=5 September 2023 |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2002/1927679.stm |date=13 April 2002 |title=Japan royals may visit Seoul |access-date=26 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030109165252/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2002/1927679.stm |archive-date=9 January 2003 |url-status=live }}</ref> The national teams of [[Japan national football team|Japan]] and [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]] qualified for the finals automatically as tournament hosts, as did the previous hosts [[France national football team|France]], the winners of the previous World Cup [[1998 FIFA World Cup|in 1998]].<ref name="2002qualifying">{{cite web |title=World Cup 2002 Qualifying |date=13 September 2018 |access-date=26 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2002q.html |first=Karel |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |last=Stokkermans |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230130011745/https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2002q.html |archive-date=30 January 2023}}</ref> The remaining 29 spots were decided through [[2002 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying rounds]] held between March 2000 and November 2001, organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]] and involving 193 teams.<ref name="2002qualifying"/><ref>{{cite book |title=Historical Dictionary of Soccer |first=Tom |last=Dunmore |edition=illustrated |publisher=Scarecrow Press |year=2011 |page=114 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9j1wbp2t1usC&pg=PA114 |isbn=978-0-81087-188-5}}</ref> In the finals, the teams were divided into eight groups of four with each team playing each other once in a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] format. The two top teams from each group advanced to a [[Single-elimination tournament|knock-out stage]]. Holders France were eliminated in the group stage in 2002, losing games against [[Senegal national football team|Senegal]] and [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] as they finished bottom of [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group A|Group A]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2002 |last=Manaschev |first=Erlan |date=3 July 2008 |access-date=26 October 2021 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2002full.html |archive-date=1 July 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701145417/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2002full.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The game was played at the [[International Stadium Yokohama|International Stadium]] in Yokohama, where three other matches in the World Cup were previously held.<ref name="stadium">{{cite web |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101127155721/https://www.nissan-stadium.jp/english/index.php |archive-date=27 November 2010 |title=2002 FIFA World Cup "Stage of a Dream" |url=http://www.nissan-stadium.jp/english/index.php |publisher=Nissan Stadium |access-date=27 June 2010 |year=2002}}</ref> The stadium was the largest in the tournament as well as the largest in Japan, seating over 70,000 spectators.<ref name="stadium"/> The aggregate attendance across all World Cup matches at the stadium was 260,000.<ref name="stadium"/>

The match ball for this game was the [[Adidas Fevernova]], which was specifically made for the World Cup.<ref name="ball">{{cite web |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040212112503/https://soccerballworld.com/Fevernova.htm |archive-date=12 February 2004 |title=Fevernova |url=http://www.soccerballworld.com/Fevernova.htm |publisher=SoccerBall World |access-date=27 June 2010 |year=2003 }}</ref> Its design was different from the normal "Tango" type of three-pointed shapes connecting each hexagon, instead introducing a different, triangle-like shape on four hexagons. This look and colour usage was based on Asian culture.<ref name="ball"/> It was manufactured using a [[syntactic foam]] layer and Adidas said should give the ball a "more precise and predictable flight path".<ref name="ball"/> Adidas said the ball was smaller and heavier than the average permitted circumference and weight, but several players criticised it for being too large and too light.<ref>{{Cite magazine |title=Fuming Over World Cup's Foam Ball |last=McClusky |first=Mark |magazine=[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]] |date=30 May 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.wired.com/2002/05/fuming-over-world-cups-foam-ball/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113000119/https://www.wired.com/2002/05/fuming-over-world-cups-foam-ball/ |archive-date=13 November 2022}}</ref> Italian goalkeeper [[Gianluigi Buffon]] called the ball "a ridiculous kiddy's [[bouncing ball]]," while Brazil's [[Edílson]] criticised the ball as being "too big and too light".<ref name="ball"/>

In the previous World Cup in 1998, Germany were eliminated by surprise debutants [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]] in the quarter-finals.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1998 finals |access-date=27 October 2021 |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/98full.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230126235117/https://www.rsssf.org/tables/98full.html |archive-date=26 January 2023}}</ref> The team suffered several injuries prior to the 2002 World Cup finals including a knee issue for [[Sebastian Deisler]], who was ruled out two days before the team departed for the tournament with an injury sustained in a [[Exhibition match|friendly match]] against [[Austria national football team|Austria]]. Midfielder [[Mehmet Scholl]] and defenders [[Christian Wörns]] and [[Jens Nowotny]] also missed the tournament due to injury.<ref name="injuries">{{cite news |title=Germany's Deisler ruled out of Cup |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/2002/world_cup/news/2002/05/20/germany_deisler_rb/ |access-date=25 June 2010 |newspaper=Sports Illustrated |date=20 May 2002 |archive-date=2 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121102080541/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/2002/world_cup/news/2002/05/20/germany_deisler_rb/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Brazil had reached the [[1998 FIFA World Cup final|final of the 1998 tournament]], where they lost 3–0 to France.<ref name="si-brazil">{{cite news|title=Brazil World Cup Preview|url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/2002/world_cup/news/2002/05/27/ws_previews/|access-date=26 June 2010|newspaper=Sports Illustrated|date=27 May 2002|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020810162737/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/2002/world_cup/news/2002/05/27/ws_previews/|archive-date=10 August 2002|url-status=dead}}</ref> Between that defeat and 2002, Brazil went through a series of [[Manager (association football)|managers]]. The first was [[Vanderlei Luxemburgo]], whose contract was terminated after the team lost another FIFA final at the [[1999 FIFA Confederations Cup|Confederations Cup]] against another host of the tournament at the time [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] in [[1999 FIFA Confederations Cup final|the final]] and were eliminated at the quarter-finals of the [[Football at the 2000 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2000 Olympic football tournament]]. He was followed by [[Émerson Leão]], who was dismissed in 2001 after winning four of eleven games in charge and with Brazil at risk of not qualifying for the World Cup for the first time.<ref name="brazil2">{{cite news |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020628031323/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/2002/world_cup/news/2002/05/30/groupc_preview_sa/ |archive-date=28 June 2002 |title=Brazilian bullies have eyes on prize |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/2002/world_cup/news/2002/05/30/groupc_preview_sa/ |access-date=26 June 2010 |newspaper=Sports Illustrated |date=31 May 2002}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Brazil dismiss their coach |last=Bellos |first=Alex |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=11 June 2001 |access-date=27 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/jun/11/newsstory.sport6 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221231222444/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/jun/11/newsstory.sport6 |archive-date=31 December 2022}}</ref> The incumbent going into the tournament was [[Luiz Felipe Scolari]], who favoured a different style of football from his predecessors which he called "bullyboy soccer".<ref name="brazil2"/> In describing the style, ''[[Soccer America]]''{{'s}} Scott French said "the object is to disrupt and destroy, foul and waste time".<ref name="brazil2"/> The two teams had met previously in several friendlies as well as the [[1980 World Champions' Gold Cup]], the [[1993 U.S. Cup]] and the [[1999 FIFA Confederations Cup]] – their most recent meeting, which resulted in a 4–0 Brazil win – but the 2002 final was their first meeting at a World Cup.<ref name="GermanyVsBrazil">{{Cite web |title=Germany national football team: record v Brazil |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/germany/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Brazil/ |work=11v11.com |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221112233143/https://www.11v11.com/teams/germany/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Brazil/ |archive-date=12 November 2022}}</ref>

This final was also the third straight for Brazil, having won the match in [[1994 FIFA World Cup final|1994]], only the second team to do so—after the Germans did that in [[1982 FIFA World Cup final|1982]] (lost), [[1986 FIFA World Cup final|1986]] (lost), and [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|1990]] (won). Brazil were aiming to become the first team to win five world titles, having won the tournament in [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]], [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]], [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]], and [[1994 FIFA World Cup|1994]]. Germany were hoping to become only the second team to win four World Cup titles, having won it as West Germany in [[1954 FIFA World Cup|1954]], [[1974 FIFA World Cup|1974]], and [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]].

