Germany were drawn in [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group E|Group E]] at the World Cup, along with [[Cameroon national football team|Cameroon]], the [[Republic of Ireland national football team|Republic of Ireland]] and [[Saudi Arabia national football team|Saudi Arabia]].<ref name="main-fifa">{{cite web|title=2002 FIFA World Cup Korea/Japan: Results |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/index.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=25 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121021164417/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/index.html |archive-date=21 October 2012 }}</ref> In their opening match against Saudi Arabia at the [[Sapporo Dome]] in the Japanese city of [[Sapporo]] they won 8–0, their biggest win in a World Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany savage Saudis |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=1 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_saudi_arabia/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029181744/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_saudi_arabia/ |archive-date=29 October 2021 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Miroslav Klose]] scored a [[hat-trick]] and was one of six players on the German team to score.<ref>{{cite web|title=Germany – Saudi Arabia |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950004/report.html |publisher=FIFA |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615212854/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950004/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> In their next game against the Republic of Ireland, Klose gave Germany a 1–0 lead which they held throughout much of the game. However, Ireland equalised through [[Robbie Keane]] in second-half [[stoppage time]]. This would prove to be the only goal conceded by Germany prior to the final.<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/><ref name="nz">{{cite news|title=Soccer: Keane saves Ireland with last-gasp goal against Germany|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/news/article.cfm?c_id=4&objectid=2045202|access-date=25 June 2010|newspaper=New Zealand Herald|date=6 June 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110511125235/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/news/article.cfm?c_id=4&objectid=2045202|archive-date=11 May 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> Needing a win to finish first in their group, Germany entered their final match against Cameroon at [[Shizuoka Stadium]] in [[Fukuroi, Shizuoka|Fukuroi]], Japan, one point ahead of the Republic of Ireland in the group. Germany went on to beat Cameroon 2–0, with goals from [[Marco Bode]] and Klose – his fifth goal of the tournament – although Germany did suffer a setback when [[Carsten Ramelow]] was [[Dismissal (sports)|sent off]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Cameroon – Germany |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950035/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=25 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615212901/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950035/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> Germany finished top of Group E with seven points (two wins and a draw), and advanced to the knockout round.<ref name="main-fifa"/>

In the round of 16, Germany faced [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]], the [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group B|Group B]] runners-up, at [[Jeju World Cup Stadium]] in the South Korean city of [[Seogwipo]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Germany – Paraguay |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950049/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=25 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615212947/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950049/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> The first half ended goalless and it remained so until the 88th minute when [[Oliver Neuville]] scored, securing a 1–0 win for Germany.<ref name="reuters">{{cite news|title=Germany edge out Paraguay|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_paraguay/default.stm|access-date=10 April 2011|work=BBC Sport|date=15 June 2002|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021014225034/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_paraguay/default.stm|archive-date=14 October 2002|url-status=live}}</ref> In their quarter-final match, Germany faced the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] who had more shots on goal, but Germany won the game 1–0 with [[Michael Ballack]] scoring in the 38th minute.<ref>{{cite web|title=Germany – USA |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950058/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=25 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615213008/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950058/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> In the semi-final, Germany faced co-hosts South Korea at the [[Seoul World Cup Stadium]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Germany – Korea Republic |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950061/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=26 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615213015/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950061/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> Like the game against Paraguay, there were no goals until late in the game, until Ballack scored the winner, hitting the rebound into the goal after goalkeeper [[Lee Woon-jae]] had saved his winner shot.<ref name="GuardianGerKor">{{Cite web |title=Germany 1 – 0 South Korea |last=Murray |first=Scott |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=25 June 2002 |access-date=26 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/25/minutebyminute.worldcupfootball2002 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113200929/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/25/minutebyminute.worldcupfootball2002 |archive-date=13 November 2022}}</ref> Four minutes before scoring, he had been shown a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] for a [[Foul (association football)|foul]] on [[Lee Chun-soo]] and was suspended for the final for receiving his second booking.<ref name="GuardianGerKor"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=CNN.com – German joy as Ballack ends Korean dream – June 25, 2002 |publisher=[[CNN]] |date=25 June 2002 |access-date=26 October 2021 |url=http://edition.cnn.com/2002/WORLD/worldcup/06/25/gerskr.report/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021020153640/http://edition.cnn.com/2002/WORLD/worldcup/06/25/gerskr.report/index.html |archive-date=20 October 2002 |url-status=live }}</ref>

