Brazil were drawn into [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group C|Group C]], along with [[China national football team|China]], [[Costa Rica national football team|Costa Rica]] and [[Turkey national football team|Turkey]].<ref name="main-fifa"/> Their first game was on 3 June against Turkey. In stoppage time at the end of the first half, Turkey's [[Hasan Şaş]] scored.<ref name="brazil3">{{cite web|title=Brazil – Turkey |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950010/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=26 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615212820/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950010/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> In the second half, Brazil's [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]] levelled the match at 1–1 in the 50th minute. Four minutes before the end of regular time, the referee awarded Brazil a [[penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] after Turkish defender [[Alpay Özalan]] fouled Brazilian striker [[Luizão (footballer, born 1975)|Luizão]], with Alpay also receiving a [[Penalty card#Red card|red card]]. [[Rivaldo]] scored from the penalty spot and the match finished 2–1 to Brazil.<ref name="brazil3"/> During the game, Turkish defender [[Hakan Ünsal]] kicked a ball towards Rivaldo which struck his thigh, but Rivaldo fell to the ground clutching his face. The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] sent the Turkish player off with a second yellow card.<ref name="Rivaldo11670">{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/04/worldcupfootball2002.sport16 |title=Scolari: Rivaldo did not cheat |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=4 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220929161444/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/04/worldcupfootball2002.sport16 |archive-date=29 September 2022}}</ref> In their second game against China at the [[Ulsan Munsu Football Stadium]] in [[Ulsan]], South Korea, Brazil won 4–0 with [[Roberto Carlos]], Rivaldo, [[Ronaldinho]] and Ronaldo all scoring.<ref name="brazil4">{{cite web|title=Brazil – China PR |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950026/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=26 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615212825/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950026/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> In their final game against Costa Rica, Brazil completed a 5–2 win. Ronaldo scored two goals, with [[Edmílson]], Rivaldo and [[Júnior (footballer, born 1973)|Júnior]] scoring the others.<ref>{{cite web|title=Costa Rica – Brazil |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950041/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=26 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615212830/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950041/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref> Brazil finished in first place in their group with nine points and eleven goals.<ref name="main-fifa"/>

In the round of 16, Brazil faced [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group H|Group H]] runners-up Belgium at the [[Noevir Stadium Kobe|Kobe Wing Stadium]] in [[Kobe]], Japan.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brazil edge past brave Belgium |work=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 October 2021 |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/brazil_v_belgium/default.stm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020805125234/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/brazil_v_belgium/default.stm |archive-date=5 August 2002 |url-status=live }}</ref> The game remained goalless until he 67th minute, when Rivaldo scored for Brazil. Ronaldo added a second goal in the 87th minute, to complete a 2–0 win for Brazil.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=43950200/match=43950054/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150216012954/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=43950200/match=43950054/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=16 February 2015 |title=Brazil – Belgium |publisher=FIFA|access-date=26 June 2010|year=2002}}</ref> Brazil faced England in the quarter-finals, with their opponents taking the lead through a goal by [[Michael Owen]] in the 23rd minute.<ref name="brazil6">{{cite web|title=England – Brazil |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950057/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=26 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100516165756/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950057/report.html |archive-date=16 May 2010 }}</ref> The scores were levelled in first half stoppage time, when Rivaldo scored.<ref name="brazil6"/> Following half-time, Ronaldinho scored for Brazil, putting them in the lead. Ronaldinho was sent off by referee [[Felipe Ramos Rizo]] seven minutes later, which meant he was suspended for the semi-final.<ref name="brazil6"/> Although they played with ten men, the Brazilians were able to prevent an equaliser from England and advanced into the semi-finals.<ref name="main-fifa"/> There, Brazil faced Turkey for the second time, at the [[Saitama Stadium 2002]] in [[Saitama (city)|Saitama]], Japan. The game was goalless until the 49th minute, when Ronaldo scored what proved to be the only goal of the match, running in the [[penalty area]] and hitting it with his toe past goalkeeper [[Rüştü Reçber]], as Brazil won 1–0.<ref name="brazil7">{{cite web|title=Brazil – Turkey |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=4395/results/matches/match=43950062/report.html |publisher=FIFA |access-date=27 June 2010 |year=2002 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100615213017/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition%3D4395/results/matches/match%3D43950062/report.html |archive-date=15 June 2010 }}</ref>

==Match==

===Pre-match===
With the exception of the suspended Ballack, who was replaced by [[Jens Jeremies]], the remainder of the team that started the semi-final against South Korea were available and selected for the final.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="FinalFifaReport"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=2002 FIFA World Cup Korea/Japan: Germany – Korea Republic |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=25 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2002korea-japan/match-center/43950061 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221201024356/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2002korea-japan/match-center/43950061 |archive-date=1 December 2022}}</ref> For Brazil, Ronaldinho returned to the team following his suspension in the semi-final. He replaced Edílson, whom [[BBC Sport]] described as having been a "poor replacement" for Ronaldinho and "almost anonymous throughout".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brazil-Turkey player ratings |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/brazil_v_turkey_semi_final/newsid_2067000/2067259.stm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021020100243/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/brazil_v_turkey_semi_final/newsid_2067000/2067259.stm |archive-date=20 October 2002 |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil's team was otherwise unchanged from the team which beat Turkey.<ref name="FinalFifaReport">{{Cite web |title=2002 FIFA World Cup Korea/Japan: Germany – Brazil |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=30 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2002korea-japan/match-center/43950064 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230129171429/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2002korea-japan/match-center/43950064 |archive-date=29 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=2002 FIFA World Cup Korea/Japan: Brazil – Turkey |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=26 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2002korea-japan/match-center/43950062#match-lineups |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029110210/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2002korea-japan/match-center/43950062#match-lineups |url-status=live }}</ref>

Brazil were considered the favourites to win the match by [[bookmaker]]s, with [[odds]] of 2–5 compared with 7–4 for Germany. Previewing the match, BBC Sport said that "both teams [had] had problematic passages to the final" but noted the two teams' experience at this level, highlighting Brazil's record number of titles as well as Germany's status as the most successful European team. The report focused on Ronaldo, who it said was "nearing redemption" having been "a shadow of his usual self after he played just a few hours after suffering a fit" in the 1998 final. It also noted that the Germans could take solace in the performances of goalkeeper [[Oliver Kahn]], who had conceded only one goal up to that point in the tournament.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World awaits historic clash |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=30 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_brazil/newsid_2075000/2075192.stm |quote=he was a shadow of his usual self after he played just a few hours after suffering a fit |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020803092503/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_brazil/newsid_2075000/2075192.stm |archive-date=3 August 2002 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Except for 1978, Brazil and Germany have participated in every final of the tournament since 1950 until the upcoming final. Despite having the most games played in the competition, they had never faced each other until now. Germany could win their first title after its reunification, and Brazil, who won their last six matches, could become the first team to win all seven matches in the tournament without requiring extra time or penalties.

[[Pierluigi Collina]] of Italy was selected to referee the final. He was described prior to the match by Stephen Wade of the [[Associated Press]] as being "regarded as game's finest referee", and {{as of|2021|lc=y}} he is regarded by many observers as the greatest of all time.<ref name="WadeCollina">{{cite news |title=Italian referee becomes a celebrity |date=29 June 2002 |newspaper=[[Montreal Gazette]] |page=E2 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |first=Stephen |last=Wade |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-gazette-italian-referee-becomes-a-ce/131373916/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |accessdate=7 September 2023 |archive-date=7 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230907070533/https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-gazette-italian-referee-becomes-a-ce/131373916/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Pierluigi Collina: The Greatest Referee In Football History |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230201125220/https://www.sportbible.com/football/news-legends-pierluigi-collina-named-the-greatest-referee-in-football-history-20210213 |archive-date=1 February 2023 |last=Kenmare |first=Jack |publisher=[[SPORTbible]] |date=13 February 2021 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.sportbible.com/football/news-legends-pierluigi-collina-named-the-greatest-referee-in-football-history-20210213}}</ref> Describing his preparation for the game, Collina said that he was "very interested in the tactics" of the two teams, so that he would be "ready to read the game". He described his selection as "unbelievable", saying that "I try to keep my feet on the ground but it is very difficult".<ref name="WadeCollina"/> The [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]] for the game were [[Leif Lindberg]] of Sweden and England's [[Philip Sharp (referee)|Philip Sharp]], while [[Hugh Dallas]] from Scotland was the [[fourth official]].<ref name="FinalFifaReport"/>

===First half===
Brazil [[Kick off (association football)|kicked off]] the game at 8pm local time (11am UTC), in front of an attendance of 69,029 with an estimated global television audience of 1.1 billion.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Blatter denies Korea conspiracy |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=24 June 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/24/worldcupfootball2002.sport17 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221202100516/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/24/worldcupfootball2002.sport17 |archive-date=2 December 2022}}</ref><ref name="11v11Report">{{Cite web |title=Brazil v Germany, 30 June 2002 |work=11v11.com |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-germany-30-june-2002-220514/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113195637/https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-germany-30-june-2002-220514/ |archive-date=13 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Advertisers kick it up for the World Cup |agency=[[Bloomberg BusinessWeek]] |work=[[NBC News]] |date=22 May 2006 |access-date=4 September 2023 |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna12917423 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230904103948/https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna12917423 |archive-date=4 September 2023}}</ref> The weather at [[Haneda Airport]], {{convert|17|km|mi|sigfig=2}} from the stadium,{{efn|Distance measured using Google Maps distance calculator, between Haneda Airport, coordinates 35.5469648°N, 139.7719668°E and Nissan Stadium, coordinates 35.5099504°N, 139.604205°E.<ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/Haneda+Airport/@35.5469648,139.7719668,14z |access-date=29 October 2021 |title=Haneda Airport}}</ref><ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/NISSAN+STADIUM/@35.5099504,139.604205,17z |access-date=20 October 2021 |title=Nissan Stadium}}</ref>}} was recorded as cloudy at the time of kick-off, with a temperature of {{convert|70|F|C|order=flip}} and 88% humidity.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Tokyo, Tokyo Prefecture, Japan Weather History – Jun 30, 2002 |publisher=[[The Weather Company]] |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/jp/tokyo/RJTT/date/2002-6-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113201039/https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/jp/tokyo/RJTT/date/2002-6-30 |archive-date=13 November 2022}}</ref> Germany were described by ''[[The Guardian]]''{{'s}} Scott Murray as "well on top" in the first fifteen minutes, and they had an opportunity to score when [[Bernd Schneider (footballer)|Bernd Schneider]] ran with the ball past [[Gilberto Silva]] before [[Cross (association football)|crossing]] into the penalty area towards Klose, where it was cleared behind by Edmílson.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rL9JCz7S8Jg |title=Germany 0–2 Brazil – Extended Highlights – 2002 FIFA World Cup Final |date=22 June 2020 |publisher=[[FIFA]] |access-date=28 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211028221131/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rL9JCz7S8Jg |archive-date=28 October 2021 |ref={{sfnRef|Germany 0–2 Brazil – Extended Highlights – 2002 FIFA World Cup Final}} |via=[[YouTube]] |time=1:03–1:20 |url-status=bot: unknown }}</ref> However, it was Brazil who had what Murray described as the "first real chance of the game" on 18 minutes.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute">{{cite news |last=Murray |first=Scott |date=30 June 2002 |title=Brazil 2–0 Germany |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/30/minutebyminute.worldcupfootball2002 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113195638/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/30/minutebyminute.worldcupfootball2002 |archive-date=13 November 2022 |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=4 June 2018}}</ref> Ronaldinho passed upfield to Ronaldo, who was [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Kahn. Ronaldo hit a shot with his left foot, but it went wide of the goal.<ref name="BBCMinuteByMinute">{{Cite web |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 June 2002 |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_brazil/newsid_2072000/2072283.stm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221105032418/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_brazil/newsid_2072000/2072283.stm |archive-date=5 November 2022 |title=World Cup final clockwatch |access-date=27 October 2021}}</ref> Schneider passed to Bode in the Brazilian penalty area on 29 minutes, but Bode was unable to control the ball. On 30 minutes Ronaldinho made another pass through to Ronaldo, who found himself with only Kahn to beat for a second time.<ref name="BBCMinuteByMinute"/> He was unable to put enough power on the shot under pressure from [[Thomas Linke]], and Kahn saved.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

Shortly before half-time, [[Torsten Frings]] sent a cross into the Brazilian penalty area from the right towards Klose, but it evaded all players and was retrieved by Neuville on the opposite side of the pitch.<ref>{{harvnb|Germany 0–2 Brazil – Extended Highlights – 2002 FIFA World Cup Final}}, 2:37–2:51</ref> He passed the ball to Jeremies outside the penalty area, who attempted a curling shot from {{convert|25|yards|m}}, but it went high and wide of the goal.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref>{{harvnb|Germany 0–2 Brazil – Extended Highlights – 2002 FIFA World Cup Final}}, 2:51–3:12</ref> [[José Kléberson|Kléberson]] then had two chances to score, putting the first wide in the 42nd minute, and hitting the bar from long range two minutes later. [[Lúcio]] had an opportunity when he received the ball in the penalty area and turned with it at his feet, but his shot was blocked. Ronaldo then missed his third scoring chance, in stoppage time, when a Roberto Carlos pass reached him after evading all the German defenders. Kahn saved Ronaldo's shot with his outstretched foot.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

===Second half===
[[File:Ronaldo 2002 cropped.jpg|thumb|Brazilian striker Ronaldo scored two goals in the second half|alt=Portrait view of Ronaldo wearing a polo shirt, taken indoors]]
One minute into the second half, Jeremies had a chance to give Germany the lead when Neuville found him in an [[Marking (association football)|unmarked]] position from a corner kick, but his header was blocked by the foot of Edmílson. They had another opportunity in the 50th minute, when Neuville struck a [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] from long range towards the corner of the goal, but Brazilian goalkeeper [[Marcos (footballer, born 1973)|Marcos]] tipped the shot onto the post.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Three minutes later, Roberto Carlos crossed into the penalty area where Gilberto Silva met the ball with a [[Header (association football)|header]], but Kahn was able to make the save. Ronaldo then hit a shot at the German goal which was blocked by Ramelow, before Klose passed to Frings at the other end and he hit his attempt over the crossbar. A shot two minutes later from [[Dietmar Hamann]] also went over the goal.<ref name="BBCMinuteByMinute"/> Then, in the 62nd minute, Schneider sent a ball into the Brazilian penalty area which Neuville was unable to reach.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

Brazil took the lead in the 67th minute with a sequence that started with Ronaldo winning the ball from Hamann in the German half of the pitch. He passed to Rivaldo, who struck a low shot towards goal. Kahn attempted to catch the shot, but he was unable to take it cleanly and it rebounded into play. Ronaldo, who had followed Rivaldo's shot, took advantage of Kahn being off balance, scoring the rebound into the bottom corner before Kahn could recover, breaking his clean sheet streak at 427 minutes. The Brazilians scored their second goal twelve minutes later, after a run from Kléberson from just beyond the halfway line led to Brazil having four attackers against Germany's three defenders. Kléberson passed towards Rivaldo, who was in the centre of the pitch just outside the German penalty area. Rivaldo let the ball go through his legs, and the pace on Kléberson's pass found Ronaldo. German player [[Gerald Asamoah]] attempted to block the shot, but Ronaldo used his first touch to take the ball away from him, and struck the ball into the bottom corner of Kahn's net with his second.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Germany had another chance in the 83rd minute when [[Oliver Bierhoff]], who had come on as a [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]], hit a first-time shot towards goal from the penalty spot, but Marcos was able to save the shot. [[Christian Ziege]] had a final shot for Germany in the third minute of stoppage time, but it was saved by Marcos and the game finished 2–0 to Brazil.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

