==Post-match==
The win marked Brazil's fifth World Cup title, which {{as of|2026|lc=y}} remains the record, ahead of Germany and [[Italy national football team|Italy]] with four titles each.<ref name="Britanica"/> Despite being at the match, the Japanese emperor [[Akihito]] did not join the ceremony of presenting the winners the World Cup trophy as it was against Japanese customs.<ref>{{cite news |last=San Martin |first=Pedro Pablo |date=30 June 2002 |title=El emperador no se rebaja a dar el trofeo |trans-title=The emperor does not stoop down to give the trophy |language=es |url=https://as.com/masdeporte/2002/06/29/polideportivo/1025324160_850215.html |work=[[Diario AS]] |access-date=4 June 2018 |archive-date=1 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201182822/https://as.com/masdeporte/2002/06/29/polideportivo/1025324160_850215.html |url-status=live }}</ref> FIFA president [[Sepp Blatter]] and former Brazilian World Cup winner [[Pelé]] gave the trophy to Brazilian captain [[Cafu]] on the pitch stage.<ref>{{harvnb|Germany 0–2 Brazil – Extended Highlights – 2002 FIFA World Cup Final}}, 11:35–12:00</ref> Ronaldo was named as the official [[man of the match]], while Kahn was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Golden Ball]] for the best individual player of the tournament as a whole – {{as of|2026|lc=y}} the only goalkeeper to receive that award.<ref>{{Cite web |title=FIFA World Cup Golden Ball: Know all winners - the complete list |last=Merrell |first=Chloe |date=18 December 2022 |access-date=27 September 2023 |url=https://olympics.com/en/news/fifa-world-cup-golden-ball-know-winners-complete-list |archive-date=16 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240816063058/https://olympics.com/en/news/fifa-world-cup-golden-ball-know-winners-complete-list |url-status=live }}</ref> He also received the Lev Yashin Award (known since 2010 as the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Glove|Golden Glove]]) for best goalkeeper at the tournament.<ref>{{Cite web |title=
2018 World Cup: What It Takes To Become The World Cup's Best Player And Be Awarded The Golden Ball |last=McMahon |first=Bobby |work=[[FIFA]] |date=25 August 2022 |access-date=27 September 2023 |url=https://www.fifa.com/fifaplus/en/watch/4juhGvb0RQbkhw1HCMJqIk |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326085737/https://www.fifa.com/fifaplus/en/watch/4juhGvb0RQbkhw1HCMJqIk |archive-date=26 March 2023}}</ref> After the tournament, FIFA conducted a video review of the incident in the first Brazil–Turkey game involving Ünsal and Rivaldo and decided to fine the Brazilian 11,670 [[Swiss franc]]s for deceiving the referee.<ref name="Rivaldo11670"/>

Scolari expressed his pleasure at the result after the match, as well as noting the happiness of the Brazilian population, saying they had "the joy of knowing we did our job, but it was full of very hard work. Brazil back as world champions – that is not something we can forget." Ronaldo said "The goals crowned my work and the work of the whole team. I worked for two years trying to recover from that injury and today God reserved this for me and the Brazilian team. I am very happy." Speaking about his role in Brazil's opening goal, Kahn said "It was my only mistake in the finals. It was ten times worse than any mistake I've ever made. There's no way I can make myself feel any better or make my mistake go away." Völler exonerated Kahn, however, saying "He has played a dream World Cup. He has made sensational saves, which no one could have expected". Regarding Germany's defeat, he said "When you lose a game the disappointment is great of course. But it is no shame to lose against a team like Brazil."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kahn: 'It was the worst mistake I've ever made' |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=30 June 2002 |access-date=28 October 2021 |url= https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/30/worldcupfootball2002.sport19 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113195638/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jun/30/worldcupfootball2002.sport19 |archive-date=13 November 2022}}</ref>

At the next World Cup in [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006]], Germany – the hosts of that tournament – reached the semi-final where they were eliminated by eventual-winners Italy. Brazil failed in the defence of their title, being eliminated in the quarter-final by France.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2006 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=29 October 2021 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2006f.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230621012658/https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2006f.html |archive-date=21 June 2023}}</ref> The next World Cup meeting between the two sides took place at the semi-finals of the [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014 tournament]], which was held in Brazil.<ref name="GermanyVsBrazil"/> In a game described by Simon Burnton of ''The Guardian'' as being "of a savagery unwitnessed against significant opposition in the tournament's history", Germany [[Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)|won the game 7–1]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup stunning moments: Germany humiliate Brazil 7–1 |last=Burnton |first=Simon |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=23 May 2013 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2018/may/23/world-cup-stunning-moments-germany-humiliate-brazil-7-1 |archive-date=13 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210913172025/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2018/may/23/world-cup-stunning-moments-germany-humiliate-brazil-7-1 |url-status=live }}</ref> They went on to win the 2014 World Cup, their sole tournament victory since the 2002 final while for Brazil, 2002 remains their most recent World Cup title as of 2026.<ref name="Britanica"/>

Over 200 nations and territories broadcast the final over radio and television.<ref name="tv">{{cite web|title=FIFA World Cup & Television |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/ffprojects/ip-401_06e_tv_2658.pdf |work=InfoPlus |publisher=FIFA |access-date=27 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070614094554/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/ffprojects/ip-401_06e_tv_2658.pdf |archive-date=14 June 2007 }}</ref> In total, 232 television channels broadcast the match, which was a new record for a World Cup Final (only later to be broken [[2006 FIFA World Cup final|in 2006]]).<ref name="tv"/> The final had the highest television audience of the entire tournament, attracting over 63 million viewers in [[Nielsen Media Research|Nielsen]]-measured countries.<ref name="tv2">{{cite web|title=Nielsen Media Research: Nearly 1.5 Billion TV Viewers Watch 2002 World Cup|url=http://goliath.ecnext.com/coms2/gi_0199-1906765/Nielsen-Media-Research-Nearly-1.html|publisher=Business Wire|access-date=27 June 2010 |date=30 July 2002|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110615044115/http://goliath.ecnext.com/coms2/gi_0199-1906765/Nielsen-Media-Research-Nearly-1.html|archive-date=15 June 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> Germany's match against South Korea was a close second, as with much of the host nation viewing the game. It was the highest-viewed non-finals match in World Cup history.<ref name="tv2"/>

